    "en":  {
               "headline":  "Strong Q3 overshadowed by debt fears and Nvidia shake-up; shares hit 52-week low",
               "technical":  "Trend: clearly down. CRWV closed at $39.51 on 2026-11-20, far below its EMA 50 ($63.98), EMA 100 ($74.24) and EMA 200 ($83.74). EMAs are moving averages that give more weight to recent prices, and these three track the short, medium and long-term trend. They are stacked in bearish order (50 below 100 below 200), the usual shape of a downtrend that has already taken hold. Momentum: MACD (a trend-momentum gauge built from two EMAs) is -6.15, sitting exactly on its signal line with a histogram of 0. Selling momentum has stopped speeding up, but no bullish crossover has happened yet. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) is 32.6, close to oversold but not there. Position: price is hugging the lower Bollinger Band at $39.47 (the bands sit 2 standard deviations either side of the 20-day average of $51.85). The day\u0027s low of $39.28 is a new 52-week low, about 71% below the 52-week high of $138.25. Volume was 76.6M shares, about 1.1x the 20-day average, so the 12% drop came on heavier-than-normal volume but not panic volume. Support: the $39–40 zone (52-week low and $40 IPO price). Resistance: about $45, then the band midline at $51.85.",
               "fundamental":  "The business is still growing fast. Q3 2026 results (reported 10 Nov) showed revenue of $3.66B, up 168%. Adjusted operating income was $379M, beating the company\u0027s own $200–260M guidance. Management raised 2026 revenue guidance to $13.0–13.3B. Backlog (signed contracts not yet billed) rose to $132.5B from $104B in June, and Anthropic is a key customer. The weak spot is the balance sheet. The GAAP net loss widened to $709M. Q3 interest expense was $846M and is guided to $1.05–1.15B for Q4. Capex is $37–39B this year, and some CoreWeave bonds have yielded above 16%. In the AI value chain, CoreWeave rents out NVIDIA GPU capacity. Its moat depends on cheap financing and early access to chips, and both look less certain after Nvidia\u0027s CEO change.",
               "news":  [
                            "10 Nov: Q3 revenue of $3.66B beat the ~$3.55B consensus. Full-year revenue guidance was raised to $13.0–13.3B, adjusted operating income guidance to $1.275–1.375B, and backlog reached $132.5B. The stock jumped 24.7% on 11 Nov.",
                            "12 Nov: Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang resigned after a scandal, and CFO Colette Kress became interim CEO. CRWV fell about 9% that day. Lenders and analysts began asking whether Nvidia\u0027s rent-back guarantees and its role in a $500B neocloud financing platform (up to $125B backstop) are still worth as much.",
                            "Financing: the CFO said the company plans less than $5B of parent-level debt through 2027 and will lean on customer prepayments and vendor financing instead. Co-founder Brian Venturo disclosed a $30M purchase of CoreWeave\u0027s unsecured bonds.",
                            "18–19 Nov: Anthropic, a major CoreWeave customer, priced its IPO at $83 (above range), raising about $81.3B. CRWV rose early on 19 Nov, then closed down 6.1% at $44.91 as traders took profits.",
                            "20 Nov: CRWV fell about 12% to a new 52-week low and briefly traded below its $40 IPO price, as debt worries and a 14% day-two drop in Anthropic shares hit AI stocks. A $350M compute commitment from Huang\u0027s foundation added to concerns about circular financing.",
                            "Analyst actions: DA Davidson cut its target to $45 from $100 and Argus cut to $100 from $150. Truist reiterated Buy with a $115 target on 19 Nov."
                        ],
               "caution":  "Bear case: CoreWeave runs on borrowed money, and that money is getting more expensive just as the market doubts Nvidia\u0027s implied support after Jensen Huang left on 12 Nov. Interest expense is heading above $1B a quarter, and free cash flow was about -$20.4B over the past year. Circular deals, such as the $350M from Huang\u0027s foundation, make it harder to judge how real the demand is. On the chart, a close below $39 would leave no recent support, and RSI is not yet oversold.",
               "opportunity":  "Bull case: revenue, backlog and guidance all rose last quarter. Management says prices are still rising faster than costs, and plans to replace expensive parent-level bonds with customer-backed project financing through 2027. Price-to-sales has fallen to about 2.5x trailing revenue (per Trefis at the 19 Nov close). The flat MACD histogram and RSI near 30 suggest selling may be running out of steam. The stock has also reacted sharply to good news: +24.7% on 11 Nov and +8–10% after Nvidia\u0027s earnings beat.",
               "risks":  [
                             "Rising interest costs ($1.05–1.15B guided for Q4) and bond yields above 16% may make expansion more expensive",
                             "Weaker implied Nvidia backstop and relationship after the CEO change",
                             "Heavy reliance on a few AI-lab customers such as Anthropic, whose own shares are volatile",
                             "Circular financing: Nvidia is a shareholder and the main supplier, plus the Huang foundation compute deal",
                             "Breaking below $39–40 support (52-week low and IPO price)",
                             "Co-founders have sold about $3.3B of stock since the March 2025 IPO"
                         ],
               "summary":  "CRWV is in a confirmed downtrend. It trades below all three EMAs, which sit in bearish order, and it is resting on the lower Bollinger Band and a new 52-week low near its $40 IPO price. Operations are strong: 168% revenue growth, a $132.5B backlog and raised guidance. November\u0027s selloff is about financing, not demand: rising interest costs, bond yields above 16% and doubts about Nvidia\u0027s backstop after Jensen Huang resigned. The moat thesis rests on scale and the Nvidia relationship. The Nvidia part is now in doubt, so the thesis moves to \u0027watch\u0027."
           },
    "th":  {
